    "result_text": "The full suite is green. Let me summarize what I implemented.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `telephoneDisplay` \u2014 the stored E.164 telephone formatted for humans \u2014 while keeping `telephone` as raw E.164.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `telephoneDisplay` property to the `Owner` schema (the OpenAPI generator produces `OwnerDto` from this spec, so the field gains a getter/setter automatically). It sits alongside the other derived fields like `displayName` and `initials`.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Added:\n   - A `@Mapping(target = \"telephoneDisplay\", expression = \"java(telephoneDisplay(owner))\")` on `toOwnerDto`.\n   - A `telephoneDisplay(Owner)` method that parses the stored E.164 value, splits off the country code (matching the same known calling codes the create endpoint normalizes against \u2014 `+61` and `+1`, longest-first), and regroups the national digits in threes: `+61412345678` \u2192 `+61 412 345 678`. It returns the raw value unchanged if telephone is null or not a `+`-prefixed digit string.\n\nThe derivation reuses the existing E.164 normalization the controller already performs on create, so `telephone` continues to be stored and returned as raw E.164.\n\n**Tests:** The full suite passes, including the new `AcceptanceTest.coreFormatsTelephoneDisplay`, which asserts `telephone = \"+61412345678\"` and `telephoneDisplay = \"+61 412 345 678\"`.",
